	Shallow Copy stores the references of objects to the original memory address.										   
	Shallow Copy reflects changes made to the new/copied object in the original object.	
	Shallow Copy stores the copy of the original object and points the references to the objects.	
	A shallow copy is faster.	
	
	ex. - 
		import copy

		# Original List
		original_list = [1, 2, [3, 4]]

		# Shallow Copy
		shallow_copy = copy.copy(original_list)

		# Modify the copied list
		shallow_copy[2][0] = 5

		# Output
		print(original_list)     # Output: [1, 2, [5, 4]]
		print(shallow_copy)      # Output: [1, 2, [5, 4]]

	
	Deep copy stores copies of the object’s value.
	Deep copy doesn’t reflect changes made to the new/copied object in the original object.
	Deep copy stores the copy of the original object and recursively copies the objects as well.
	Deep copy is comparatively slower.
	
	ex. -
	
		import copy

		# Original List
		original_list = [1, 2, [3, 4]]

		# Deep Copy
		deep_copy = copy.deepcopy(original_list)

		# Modify the copied list
		deep_copy[2][0] = 5

		# Output
		print(original_list)     # Output: [1, 2, [3, 4]]
		print(deep_copy)         # Output: [1, 2, [5, 4]]



